### How do you use a custom ForkJoinPool?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool;

public class CustomPoolDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        List<String> accounts = List.of("SAV2001", "SAV2002", "CUR3001", "SAV2003");

        ForkJoinPool customPool = new ForkJoinPool(4);
        try {
            List<String> result = customPool.submit(() ->
                accounts.parallelStream()
                    .filter(a -> a.startsWith("SAV"))
                    .toList()
            ).get();
            System.out.println("Custom pool result: " + result);
        } finally {
            customPool.shutdown();
        }
    }
}
```

### What are the caveats?

| Caveat | Problem | Fix |
|--------|---------|-----|
| Ordering | forEach scrambles output | Use forEachOrdered or toList() |
| Small data | Overhead exceeds benefit < 10K | Use sequential stream |
| Shared state | Race conditions | Use collect instead of forEach+add |
| Common pool | Slow stream blocks others | Use custom ForkJoinPool |
| Bad source | LinkedList splits in O(n) | Use ArrayList or array |
| I/O-bound | Threads sit idle | Use CompletableFuture or virtual threads |

### When should you use parallel streams?

Use parallel streams when you have more than 10,000 elements, CPU-bound work per element, independent elements with no shared state, a well-splittable source like ArrayList, and order is not critical or you use collect/toList which preserves encounter order.

### What should a senior engineer know?

For production systems, always use a custom ForkJoinPool for critical parallel pipelines to avoid starving the common pool. Profile with JMH before assuming parallel is faster. Consider virtual threads (Java 21) for I/O-bound parallelism instead of parallel streams.
